LINES = [
 "Note Atlas: a local-first research workspace, built on retrieval-augmented generation.",
 "Commercial language models process every prompt on remote servers. Research notes and unpublished papers leave the device, where they may be logged, retained, or leaked.",
 "Retrieval-augmented generation changes how a model answers. Instead of relying on what it memorised during training, the model is handed the most relevant passages from your own documents, and must answer from them, with citations.",
 "Note Atlas keeps the entire pipeline on one machine. Backend, vector stores, and the Ollama model server all run on localhost, across three modules: Boards, Files, and the Research Track.",
 "On a Board, each note is a card on a canvas. A local model extracts its topics, embeddings suggest related notes as strings, and a lasso-scoped Ask answers only from the selected notes.",
 "Board Ask retrieves note chunks by semantic similarity, and boosts exact keyword matches. When nothing scores above the threshold, it does not guess. It declines.",
 "Files is a classic RAG pipeline. Documents are split into five-hundred-character chunks, embedded into Chroma D B, and the three nearest chunks ground a Phi-3 answer, cited by page.",
 "Each enhancement can be switched on independently: Word documents, keyword search fused with vector search, conversational memory, and a model picker. The same pipeline also powers a Streamlit laboratory app.",
 "The Research Track goes further. Every uploaded paper enters a resumable, four-step job queue.",
 "Step one: parse. A local GROBID service converts the PDF into structured XML: metadata, sections, and every reference, which is stored as a lead.",
 "Step two: extract. The local model records the research question, method, key results, scope, and each contribution, as a separate claim.",
 "Step three: limitations. Each limitation must carry a verbatim quote. It is kept only if that quote exists in the paper, and a second check confirms it admits a weakness.",
 "Step four: lineage. References are ranked by where they are cited, and the model scores how much the paper builds on each. The strongest become parent papers, revealing siblings and ancestors.",
 "Completed extractions are indexed for Research Ask. It searches fields and claims, never the full text, and every citation names the exact claim it came from.",
 "Optional scholarly lookups enrich the library with metadata. They send only titles and identifiers, never the text, and can be switched off.",
 "Note Atlas also speaks the Model Context Protocol. A small local server bridges Claude Code and Claude Desktop to the running backend, over standard input and output.",
 "It exposes nine tools: boards and notes, semantic search, Atlas's own grounded Ask, the research library, and Research Ask. Every tool is read-only, except creating a note.",
 "This bridge is optional. Claude itself runs in the cloud, so anything it reads through these tools leaves the machine. The local pipeline never depends on it.",
 "Across every module, accuracy comes from constraint, not model size. Answers cite their sources, weak retrieval is declined rather than guessed, and every limitation is backed by a verified quote.",
 "The thesis takeaway: high-accuracy, grounded answers, with no internet connection required, and no document ever leaving the device.",
]
